---
/* THE NUMBERS

   Three measurements, none of them ours, read in the order they were taken.
   The point is not that speaking is fast: it is that speaking was slower until
   the correction problem was solved, which is the problem this product is
   built around. Every figure below was read out of the paper it belongs to,
   not off a site quoting it. All bars are scaled against 161.2 wpm, the
   fastest reading here.

   Zero JS in the markup. The bars carry --f as an inline fraction and animate
   off .is-in; the only thing a script adds is the count-up on the figures, and
   the server already prints every one of them at its final value. */
import { HEAT_CELLS, HEAT_LEGEND, OWN, WEEKS } from '../lib/heat';

/* THE FOUR TILES CARRY WHAT PRODUCTION'S FOUR TILES CARRY.

   A figure and a label are not a reading. `Home.tsx` prints a foot under every
   one of these and the feet are load-bearing rather than decorative: `median,
   N dictations` is what stops a rate from being a mean over a denominator that
   silently skipped half the records, and the two-line foot under Time saved is
   ADR 0182, which says outright that the baseline is not context about that
   reading, it IS the reading -- the same four weeks are 43 minutes at 40 wpm
   and 15 at 60.

   This page printed four figures with four descriptive phrases under them and
   none of the three things that make a figure mean something: no median, no
   span, no baseline. `9h 40m` saved over an unstated period against an
   unstated comparison is the shape of a marketing number, which is the one
   shape this section exists to avoid.

   Every value below is read out of ../lib/heat, where three of the four are
   computed over the same array the field above them draws. */
---
<section class="sec plane" id="numbers">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec__head rise">
      <h2>It counts what it did <em>for you</em>.</h2>
      <p>That speaking beats typing is not our finding, and for most of the time computers
        could listen at all it was not even true. The three measurements below are other
        people's.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="tp rise" data-d="1">
      <li class="tp__step">
        <div class="tp__head">
          <span class="tp__year mono">1999</span>
          <b class="tp__title">Speaking lost.</b>
        </div>
        <div class="tp__pair">
          <div class="tp__m" style="--f:.084">
            <span class="tp__k mono">spoken</span>
            <span class="tp__track"><i></i></span>
            <b class="tp__n" data-n="13.6" data-dec="1">13.6<i>wpm</i></b>
          </div>
          <div class="tp__m tp__m--win" style="--f:.202">
            <span class="tp__k mono">typed</span>
            <span class="tp__track"><i></i></span>
            <b class="tp__n" data-n="32.5" data-dec="1">32.5<i>wpm</i></b>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="tp__src mono">Transcription by voice against keyboard and mouse, 24 users,
          three commercial recognisers. Karat, Halverson, Horn and Karat, <i>Patterns of Entry
          and Correction in Large Vocabulary Continuous Speech Recognition Systems</i>, CHI
          1999. Figures as reported in Ruan et al. 2016.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="tp__step">
        <div class="tp__head">
          <span class="tp__year mono">2016</span>
          <b class="tp__title">It turned over.</b>
        </div>
        <div class="tp__pair">
          <div class="tp__m tp__m--win tp__m--hi" style="--f:1">
            <span class="tp__k mono">spoken</span>
            <span class="tp__track"><i></i></span>
            <b class="tp__n" data-n="161.2" data-dec="1">161.2<i>wpm</i></b>
          </div>
          <div class="tp__m" style="--f:.332">
            <span class="tp__k mono">typed</span>
            <span class="tp__track"><i></i></span>
            <b class="tp__n" data-n="53.5" data-dec="1">53.5<i>wpm</i></b>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="tp__src mono">English short messages, 32 participants, with 20.4 percent
          fewer errors than the keyboard. Measured on a phone. Ruan, Wobbrock, Liou, Ng and
          Landay, <i>Comparing Speech and Keyboard Text Entry for Short Messages in Two
          Languages on Touchscreen Phones</i>, 2016.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="tp__step">
        <div class="tp__head">
          <span class="tp__year mono">2018</span>
          <b class="tp__title">The desk, checked.</b>
        </div>
        <div class="tp__pair">
          <div class="tp__m" style="--f:.320">
            <span class="tp__k mono">typed, physical keyboard</span>
            <span class="tp__track"><i></i></span>
            <b class="tp__n" data-n="51.6" data-dec="1">51.6<i>wpm</i></b>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="tp__src mono">Mean 51.56, SD 20.2, across 168,000 participants. Dhakal,
          Feit, Kristensson and Oulasvirta, <i>Observations on Typing from 136 Million
          Keystrokes</i>, CHI 2018.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="tp__close rise" data-d="1">The 2016 study ran on a phone, and that is the
      objection worth answering rather than hiding: its touchscreen keyboard came out at
      53.5 wpm, and a physical keyboard measured across 168,000 people comes out at 51.6.
      The typing side barely moves. What moved is the other side, and what held it back in
      1999 was <b>correction, not speed</b>. That is the half the modes do.</p>

    <p class="nums__k rise" data-d="1">These five are yours, and every one of them is read
      off your own disk.</p>

    {/* The field is a figure, not a texture, so it sits with the other four
        rather than closing the page. What it counts is dictations per day,
        which is the one of the five that only makes sense as a shape. */}
    <div class="own lit rise" data-d="1">
      <div class="own__field">
        <div class="own__head">
          <div>
            <b class="own__t">Days you dictated</b>
            <span class="own__sub">every day you spoke to it, and how much</span>
          </div>
          <span class="own__legend mono" aria-hidden="true">less{
            HEAT_LEGEND.map((l) => <i class="own__c" data-l={l}></i>)
          }more</span>
        </div>
        {/* ONE PANEL, NOT 364. Every cell carries its own reading as text and
            a single node draws it, because a tooltip element per cell is three
            hundred and sixty-four subtrees in the served HTML for one that can
            ever be visible. The panel is a child of the field rather than the
            grid so it is not clipped by the grid's own box. */}
        <div class="own__grid" style={`--cols:${WEEKS}`}
             role="img"
             aria-label="An activity field of one year. Columns are weeks, rows are weekdays, and brighter cells are days with more dictations.">
          {HEAT_CELLS.map((c) => (
            <i class="own__c" data-l={c.level} style={`--i:${c.week}`} data-tip={c.tip} tabindex="-1"></i>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="own__tip" role="tooltip" data-own-tip="" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <p class="own__foot mono">One column is a week, one row is a weekday, Monday at the
          top. Hover a day for what it holds: the dictations, the words, the two clocks, and the
          meetings and uploads that landed on it. Read from the same history file the four figures
          below are computed from.</p>
      </div>

      <ul class="nums">
        {/* A SPEAKING rate since ADR 0177, and a median: the foot names what it
            was computed over, because a rate whose denominator silently skipped
            half the records is a plausible wrong number. */}
        <li class="tile">
          <b data-n={OWN.rate} data-delay="120">{OWN.rate}</b>
          <span class="tile-label">Words per minute</span>
          <span class="tile-foot">median, over {OWN.timed.toLocaleString('en-US')} dictations</span>
        </li>
        {/* The one figure derived from an assumption rather than a measurement,
            so the foot carries the assumption. Rolling four weeks, not a
            lifetime: a lifetime figure stops being something a reader can
            hold. */}
        <li class="tile">
          <b><span data-n={OWN.savedHours} data-delay="200">{OWN.savedHours}</span><i>h</i>
             <span data-n={OWN.savedMinutes} data-delay="200">{OWN.savedMinutes}</span><i>m</i></b>
          <span class="tile-label">Time saved</span>
          <span class="tile-foot">an estimate, over {OWN.savedSpan}<br />against {OWN.baseline} wpm typing</span>
        </li>
        {/* From you stopping to the text being ready (ADR 0181), in seconds
            (ADR 0191), and a median over every run rather than a best case. */}
        <li class="tile">
          <b><span data-n={OWN.turnaround} data-dec="1" data-delay="280">{OWN.turnaround}</span><i>s</i></b>
          <span class="tile-label">Turnaround</span>
          <span class="tile-foot">median wait, over every run</span>
        </li>
        {/* Measured on the delivered text (ADR 0180), which works on every lane
            and offline. The runs too short to name a language are in no bucket
            at all, and the foot says so rather than telling a bilingual reader
            they speak one language (ADR 0186). */}
        <li class="tile">
          <b data-n={OWN.languages} data-delay="360">{OWN.languages}</b>
          <span class="tile-label">Languages</span>
          <span class="tile-foot">mostly {OWN.topLanguage}, {OWN.topShare} percent<br />
            measured on {OWN.measured.toLocaleString('en-US')} of {OWN.dictations.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="slab__note rise" data-d="2" style="max-width:44rem">A constructed example,
      not an average of anybody. What is real is that the app measures these five things,
      out of a file you can open.</p>
  </div>
</section>
